.TH IOTRACKER 1 "November 2026" "Operating Systems I" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
iotracker \- monitor a process's real-time I/O activity
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B iotracker
[\fB\-i\fR \fIsec\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIcount\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIpath\fR]... [\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-q\fR]
.B \-p
.I pid
.PP
.B iotracker
[\fB\-i\fR \fIsec\fR] [\fB\-n\fR \fIcount\fR] [\fB\-w\fR \fIpath\fR]... [\fB\-f\fR] [\fB\-q\fR]
.B \-\-
.I command
[\fIargs\fR...]
.PP
.B iotracker \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B iotracker
reports the I/O activity of one process while it runs. It either attaches
to an existing process (\fB\-p\fR) or launches a command and follows it
until it exits.
.PP
Every interval it reads the kernel's I/O accounting for the process from
.I /proc/PID/io
and prints one line with:
.TP 12
.B read/s, write/s
bytes passed to read and write system calls per second (includes data
served from the page cache, pipes and terminals).
.TP
.B disk\-rd/s, disk\-wr/s
bytes per second that actually had to be fetched from, or sent to, the
storage layer.
.TP
.B rd\-sys, wr\-sys
number of read and write system calls in the interval.
.PP
With
.BR \-w ,
.B iotracker
uses
.BR inotify (7)
to print file events (OPEN, ACCESS, MODIFY, CLOSE_WRITE, CLOSE_NOWRITE,
CREATE, DELETE, MOVED_FROM, MOVED_TO) on the given files or directories as
they happen.
.PP
With
.BR \-f ,
it follows the offset of every regular file the process has open (from
.IR /proc/PID/fdinfo )
and prints how each offset moves. A file whose offset only moves forward is
reported as
.BR sequential ;
a file whose offset ever moves backwards (a seek) is reported as
.BR random .
.PP
When monitoring ends (the process exits, the sample limit is reached, or
.B iotracker
receives SIGINT or SIGTERM), a summary is printed with total and average
traffic, peak rates, per-file inotify event counts, the access pattern of
each file, and the exit status of a launched command.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " pid"
Monitor the existing process
.IR pid .
Cannot be combined with a command.
.TP
.BI \-i " sec"
Sampling interval in seconds, between 0.05 and 3600. Fractions such as
0.5 are allowed. Default: 1.
.TP
.BI \-n " count"
Stop after
.I count
samples. A launched command is then terminated with SIGTERM. Default:
run until the process exits.
.TP
.BI \-w " path"
Watch a file or directory with inotify. May be given up to 32 times.
.TP
.B \-f
Follow open file offsets and report sequential or random access.
.TP
.B \-q
Quiet: print only the final summary.
.TP
.B \-h
Show usage information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-
End of options. Everything after it is the command to launch.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Monitoring finished normally (including after SIGINT or SIGTERM).
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error: the process does not exist, its I/O counters cannot be
read, the command cannot be started, or a path cannot be watched.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Watch a copy, sampling twice a second:
.PP
.RS
.B iotracker \-i 0.5 \-\- cp big.iso /tmp/copy.iso
.RE
.PP
Attach to a running process for 10 samples:
.PP
.RS
.B iotracker \-p 1234 \-n 10
.RE
.PP
Show file events in a directory and the access pattern of open files:
.PP
.RS
.B iotracker \-f \-w ./data \-\- ./myprogram
.RE
.PP
Print only the summary:
.PP
.RS
.B iotracker \-q \-\- tar czf backup.tgz ./project
.RE
.SH IMPLEMENTATION
.BR poll (2)
waits on a
.BR timerfd_create (2)
timer and the inotify descriptor at the same time.
.BR ioctl (2)
with FIONREAD sizes the inotify read buffer exactly, and with TIOCGWINSZ
fits output lines to the terminal width.
A launched command is started with
.BR fork (2)
and
.BR execvp (3)
under
.BR ptrace (2)
with PTRACE_O_TRACEEXIT, so the kernel stops it just before it exits and
the final counters can still be read; signals sent to the command are
passed through unchanged.
.BR sigaction (2)
handles SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGCHLD, and the previous handlers are
restored on return so the command can run inside the Custom Shell.
.SH LIMITATIONS
.IP \(bu 2
.I /proc/PID/io
counts only the process itself (all its threads), not child processes it
creates. Monitoring
.B "sh \-c 'cat file'"
measures the shell, not
.BR cat .
Launch the program that does the I/O directly.
.IP \(bu 2
inotify is path based: events on watched paths are reported no matter
which process caused them.
.IP \(bu 2
File offsets are only seen at sample times, so a file opened and closed
between two samples does not appear in the
.B \-f
report. Use a smaller interval for short-lived files.
.IP \(bu 2
Disk columns can stay at 0 when data is served from the page cache, or
when writes are still waiting to be flushed by the kernel.
.IP \(bu 2
Monitoring another user's process requires root. A launched command
cannot already be traced by a debugger.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR inotify (7),
.BR proc (5),
.BR poll (2),
.BR ioctl (2),
.BR ptrace (2),
.BR timerfd_create (2),
.BR iotop (8),
.BR strace (1)
